ce drawn independently from p(x | y = +1) and ri is the positive confidence given by ri = p(y = +1|xi). Since we only have positive instances, we cannot directly minimize the empirical risk in Eq. 1. However, Ishida et al. (2018) showed that the following theorem holds. Theorem 2.2. (Ishida et al., 2018) The classification risk can be expressed as\nR(g) = \u03c0+E+ [ \u2113(g(x)) + 1\u2212r(x)r(x) \u2113(\u2212g(x)) ] (2)\nif we have r(x) = p(y = +1|x) \u0338= 0 for all x sampled from p(x). E+ is the expectation over the positive distribution p(x|y = +1) and \u03c0+ = p(y = +1).\nSince the expectation is over p(x|y = +1), we do not have to prepare negative data. Although \u03c0+ is usually unknown, we do not need this knowledge since \u03c0+ can be regarded as a constant when minimizing Eq. 2 to learn a classifier."
