        "heading": "6 CONCLUSION",
        "text": "We proposed a model-free and instance-free Bayes error estimation method that is unbiased and consistent. We showed how we can similarly estimate it with uncertainty labels, noisy soft labels, or positive-confidence. In experiments, we demonstrated how the proposed method could be helpful when we want to compare the SOTA performance for benchmark datasets with the Bayes error.\nAlthough we did not detect strong evidence of test set overfitting in Sec. 5.2 and Sec. 5.3, we are interested in investigating other benchmark datasets in the future. A current limitation is that we focus on binary classification. Since binary classification is popular in industry, e.g., click prediction, fraud detection, anomaly detection, churn prediction, we believe estimating the Bayes error for binary classification problems is interesting on its own. However, in the future we are interested in working on the multi-class case (and the multi-label case we discussed in Sec. 5.3).\nEthics statement: Constructing soft labels may rely on collecting human annotations. This may potentially have a negative impact, e.g., cost of the annotations. We are hoping to lower the cost of annotations in our future work. We will only publish our annotations we collected in Sec. 5.3 in a way that does not contain any private information, e.g., only the category counts for each images and drop all other worker-related information.\nReproducibility statement: In the synthetic experiments, we used four setups: A, B, C, D. A is a 10-dimensional isotropic setup with means \u00b5+ = [0, . . . , 0]\u22a4 and \u00b5\u2212 = [1, . . . , 1]\u22a4 with identity matrices for the covariance matrices. B is the same as A except that the dimension is doubled to 20. C and D are 2-dimensional anisotropic setups and is borrowed from the first two synthetic datasets in Ishida et al. (Ishida et al., 2018), where the code is provided in https: //github.com/takashiishida/pconf.\nWe used data and code provided in https://cvjena.github.io/cifair/ for ciFAIR10 (Barz & Denzler, 2020). We used data and code provided in https://github.com/ jcpeterson/cifar-10h for CIFAR-10H (Peterson et al., 2019). We used data and code provided in https://github.com/modestyachts/CIFAR-10.1 for CIFAR-10.1 (Recht et al., 2019). We used data provided in https://www.cs.toronto.edu/~kriz/cifar. html for CIFAR-10. We are not aware of any personally identifiable information or offensive content in the datasets we used. We also collected annotations by using Amazon Mechanical Turk (Buhrmester et al., 2016). We explained the details of how we collected the annotations in Sec. 5.3, and we plan to remove all personally identifiable information when we publish the data after acceptance of the paper.\nWe used https://github.com/mrtnoshad/Bayes_Error_Estimator for the ensemble method (Noshad et al., 2019). We partially used https://github.com/ takashiishida/flooding for training classifiers in synthetic experiments with Gaussian distributions. We used https://github.com/huyvnphan/PyTorch_CIFAR10 for VGG11, ResNet50, and DenseNet169 models trained on CIFAR-10. We used https://github. com/jeonsworld/ViT-pytorch to fine-tune ViT and downloaded pretrained weights from https://console.cloud.google.com/storage/browser/vit_models;tab= objects?pli=1&prefix=&forceOnObjectsSortingFiltering=false. All of our experiments were conducted with a single Mac Pro except when we trained ResNet-18 in Sec. 5.3 and fine-tuned ViT in Sec. 5.2, which was when we utilized Google Colab Pro+.\nThe details of the four binary datasets that are used in Sec. 5.2 is as follows.\n\u2022 Animals vs. artifacts: we divide the dataset into cat, deer, dog, frog, and horse for the positive class and plane, car, ship, truck, and bird for the negative class.\n\u2022 Land vs. other (water/sky): we divide the dataset into car, truck, cat, deer, dog, horse for the positive class and plane, ship, bird, and frog for the negative class.\n\u2022 Odd vs. even: we divide the dataset into odd and even class numbers, with plane, bird, deer, frog, and ship for the positive class and car, cat, dog, horse, and truck for the negative class.\n\u2022 First five vs. last five: we divide the dataset into the first five and last five classes based on the class number, with plane, car, bird, cat, and deer for the positive class and dog, frog, horse, ship, and truck for the negative class."
